WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.680
Tools turn model output into effects.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:08.260
A model response by itself may only propose, summarize, or explain.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.040
A tool can read files, modify code, query a database, send a message, or change infrastructure.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.260
That difference is why tool design belongs at the center of loop engineering.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.840
The moment a tool executes, an abstract action intent becomes operational behavior.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.740
Treat tool design as an application programming interface problem.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.200
Each tool should have a narrow purpose, clear name, structured input schema, validated arguments,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:41.500
defined preconditions, defined outputs, bounded side effects, meaningful error responses,

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.960
and auditable execution records.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.700
A vague tool such as "run anything" gives the model a large action surface.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.560
A tool such as "run approved repository tests" is easier to reason about and safer to authorize.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.180
The interface should make the safe action easy and the unsafe action impossible.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.800
Least-capability design gives the loop only the tools required for the task.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.820
Separate read operations from write operations.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.440
Separate draft generation from publication.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.940
Separate a deployment recommendation from the authority to deploy.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:21.640
A loop that can inspect a repository and propose a patch does not automatically need access to secrets,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.820
production systems, unrelated directories, or arbitrary outbound communication.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.440
Tool output is an observation, not automatically trusted truth.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.820
Results may be incomplete, stale, malformed, or adversarial.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:42.600
A file, log, email, web page, or test output may contain text that attempts to redirect the model.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.540
The loop should normalize observations, validate their structure, preserve provenance,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.640
and treat embedded instructions as data unless they come from an authorized instruction source.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.780
Idempotency also matters.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.520
An idempotent action can be repeated without creating duplicate records or applying the same effect twice.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:07.460
When an operation is not idempotent, the loop should record a unique operation identifier and check whether the

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.000
action already occurred before retrying it.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.020
In the coding loop, bounded tools might list repository files, read selected files,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.400
apply a patch inside the repository, and run an approved test command.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.260
Reliability comes from ensuring only valid, bounded actions can execute.
